    {"id":"puppies-landing","type":"content","label":"Puppies","root":true,"position":{"x":0,"y":120},"width":460,"height":280,"ports":[{"id":"root","key":"root","label":"root","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":180},{"id":"detail","key":"detail","label":"why treats?","direction":"output","dataType":"navigation","angle":0}],"handles":[{"id":"root","portId":"root","label":"root","direction":"bidirectional","dataType":"any","angle":180,"position":{"side":"left","offset":0.5}},{"id":"detail","portId":"detail","label":"why treats?","direction":"output","dataType":"navigation","angle":0,"position":{"side":"right","offset":0.5}}],"visible":true,"showLabel":true,"data":{"identity":{"graphId":"getting-started-puppies"},"content":{"kind":"markdown","value":"# 🐶 Puppies\n\nThis is the **landing content** of a real Twilite graph.\n\nThe graph has its own detail, summary, icon, and glyph surfaces, plus content you can move through.\n\nFollow the connection to see one reason puppies and treats belong together."}}},
    {"id":"puppies-reinforcement","type":"content","label":"Why treats?","root":false,"position":{"x":620,"y":120},"width":420,"height":260,"ports":[{"id":"root","key":"root","label":"root","direction":"input","dataType":"navigation","angle":180},{"id":"workspace","key":"workspace","label":"keep the context","direction":"output","dataType":"navigation","angle":0}],"handles":[{"id":"root","portId":"root","label":"root","direction":"input","dataType":"navigation","angle":180,"position":{"side":"left","offset":0.5}},{"id":"workspace","portId":"workspace","label":"keep the context","direction":"output","dataType":"navigation","angle":0,"position":{"side":"right","offset":0.5}}],"visible":true,"showLabel":true,"data":{"identity":{"graphId":"getting-started-puppies"},"content":{"kind":"markdown","value":"# Treats are useful\n\nDogs can learn associations quickly. A small reward can reinforce a behavior and make training clearer.\n\nThe important onboarding point is structural: this content did not need to be stuffed into the first node.\n\nNow take the next step: keep this graph present while you open the workspace lesson alongside it."}}},
